{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The largest of the three consecutive even integers is 48, because the three integers are 44, 46, and 48, which sum to 138.","counterconditions":["If the three numbers were not required to be even (e.g., any three consecutive integers), the offset structure would change and the largest would differ.","If the problem did not specify exactly three integers, the sum-of-mean principle would not apply in this form."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Let the three consecutive even integers be n-2, n, and n+2. Their sum is 3n. Setting 3n = 138 yields n = 46. The three integers are therefore 44, 46, and 48, making the largest 48. This is the covering principle: for any three evenly spaced numbers, the sum is three times the middle value, so the middle value is the arithmetic mean (138/3 = 46).","optional_refs":["f2747d4910edd626ac62f9e6fed9cbc748a23b23ef8bc44609ad1e4377235aa8","2316baad7b12326eaa49a37bacb6e6b07c11b9fe82018e24c78fe7496ed5397a","b03c164328561538ee0e59e2ac7fb421e01732d1f8f46a5a31720980ea0f5719","e7bb307edea5a70dc2260902793322c5be8265d5da5cfaac6d8073053a463e0d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.99},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The largest integer is 48, derived by first finding the arithmetic mean and then stepping one even interval above it.","counterconditions":["If the spacing between consecutive terms were not 2 (i.e., not even integers), the +2 step would not recover the largest.","If the problem involved a different count of integers, the mean would not equal the middle term."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The arithmetic mean of any three evenly spaced values equals the middle value. The mean of the sum 138 is 138/3 = 46. Since the integers are consecutive even integers, the spacing is 2, so the largest is 46 + 2 = 48. This isolates the mean-step principle as the covering law.","optional_refs":["2316baad7b12326eaa49a37bacb6e6b07c11b9fe82018e24c78fe7496ed5397